## The Challenge
When I first read the challenge, I saw a long string of numbers like `0x70 0x69 0x63 0x6f ...`. The description said:
> The ‘0x’ prefix in front of these numbers tells you that the number is in hexadecimal base. 
You could look up an ASCII table and solve this problem manually, but I would recommend looking at the hints for this problem for a more efficient solution.
I knew `0x` meant hexadecimal. I also knew ASCII maps numbers to characters. But I had no idea how many characters there were, and I didn't want to manually convert 50+ hex values. That sounded like torture.

## First Thoughts – "This Should Be Easy"
I thought, *"Okay, hex to ASCII. I can just use Python."* So I opened my terminal and tried:

```python
>>> hex_string = "0x70 0x69 0x63 0x6f ..."
>>> bytes.fromhex(hex_string).decode()
